1. WHAT IS OBJECT-ORIENTED PROGRAMMING (OOP)?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer

Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm
that organizes software around objects rather than functions.

An object contains

• State (Data/Attributes)
• Behavior (Methods)

OOP is based on four fundamental principles

• Encapsulation
• Inheritance
• Polymorphism
• Abstraction

Advantages

• Code Reusability
• Better Security (Data Hiding)
• Easy Maintenance
• Scalability
• Modularity


======================================================================
2. FOUR PILLARS OF OOP
----------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Encapsulation

Definition

Wrapping data and methods into a single unit (class)
and restricting direct access using private variables.

Real World Example

Bank Account

Balance is private.
Users access it only through deposit() and withdraw().

--------------------------------------------------

2. Inheritance

Definition

Acquiring properties and methods of a parent class
using the extends keyword.

Purpose

Code Reusability

Real World Example

Vehicle

↓

Car

--------------------------------------------------

3. Polymorphism

Definition

One Interface, Many Forms

Same method behaves differently for different objects.

Real World Example

One Person

↓

Father

Son

Husband

--------------------------------------------------

4. Abstraction

Definition

Hiding implementation details and exposing only the
essential functionality.

Real World Example

Driving a Car

User knows

Accelerator

Brake

Steering

User doesn't know

Engine Working


======================================================================
