1. WHAT IS ENCAPSULATION?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer

Encapsulation is the process of wrapping data (fields)
and methods into a single unit called a class while
restricting direct access to the data.

It is achieved by

• Declaring variables as private
• Providing public Getter and Setter methods

Benefits

• Data Hiding
• Security
• Controlled Access
• Better Maintainability
• Better Modularity


======================================================================
2. REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE OF ENCAPSULATION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Example

Bank Account

The account balance is private.

Users cannot directly modify it.

Instead,

Bank provides methods like

getBalance()

deposit()

withdraw()

These methods control how the balance is accessed
and modified.

This is Encapsulation.


======================================================================
3. WHY DO WE USE GETTERS AND SETTERS?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Getter

Used to read the value of a private variable.

Setter

Used to update the value of a private variable.

Purpose

• Controlled Access
• Data Hiding
• Validation
• Security
• Maintainability

Example

private int age;

public int getAge()

public void setAge(int age)


======================================================================
4. ENCAPSULATION PROGRAM
----------------------------------------------------------------------

```java
class Bank {

    private String name;
    private int balance;

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }

    public void setBalance(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Bank b = new Bank();

        b.setName("Vishal");
        b.setBalance(5000);

        System.out.println(b.getName());
        System.out.println(b.getBalance());
    }
}